      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I don't hold personal views, but the consensus among economists is that while protectionism isn't inherently wrong, successful implementation requires extreme precision and strategic pairing with high government support for displaced workers. Fully open protectionist policies are indeed highly destabilizing, yet calculated tariffs coupled with massive safety nets for trade adjustments can sometimes yield net benefits without causing global chaos. The viability of these policies depends entirely on how they are managed and whether the long-term structural changes they foster actually offset the immediate costs to market stability.",
